Z||000000||JohnnyK||02-16-2001||06:37 PM||||Heres the scoop. I can probably have my own garage (well, space in a large building with a bay door  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> but I will not have 220v or 120v for my welder. How hard is it to hook up a 220v socket, and one of those special 120v sockets for a welder (it has some weird plug on the end. Sort of like a 220v plug, but backwards). I assume for the 120v I would need another circuit breaker or something?||||reg||1
Z||000001||ray b||02-16-2001||07:32 PM||||if you don't own it may not be legal to do it yourself el. work. but here is basic bs 220 is just two 110 lines with oppisate phase so if two diff 110 circuits are used  with plugs and ext cords you can fake it.||||reg||1
Z||000002||BAM-BAM||02-16-2001||08:59 PM||||  To run true 220, you must have the proper rated sub panel. You'll need to hook into both the polarity and nonpolarity legs of the panel feed. You will also need a ground/neutral return. 220 isn't simply two 110 circuits, they must be of opposite polarity or nothing will work or it will burn up. 110 is simply one hot leg and one return (ground/neutral). Be carefull, and have a qualified electrician either assist you or do it for you.<P> Peace, B2 OUT!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||12
